── linux ──────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #1281  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: vsetvli with reserved VSEW/VLMUL must zero vl to prevent vector instructions from executing with a stale element count under an illegal vtype configuration
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kernel/signal.c:115-143  __restore_v_state
      Copies vtype, vl, vstart, vcsr from user-controlled signal frame via __copy_from_user with ZERO validation (line 129). A malicious signal frame can set vtype to a reserved/illegal encoding (e.g. VLMUL=4, SEW > ELEN) with non-zero vl. On deferred restore (TIF_RISCV_V_DEFER_RESTORE set at line 126), __vstate_csr_restore issues 'vsetvl x0, vl, vtype' with these attacker-controlled values. Unlike ptrace (which has invalid_ptrace_v_csr), no equivalent check exists here. This is the most direct path for userspace to inject inconsistent vtype/vl into kernel V state.
  [BUG] arch/riscv/kvm/vcpu_vector.c:165-203  kvm_riscv_vcpu_set_reg_vector
      Only validates vlenb (lines 181-193), but performs ZERO validation on vtype, vl, vstart, or vcsr. A VMM can independently set vtype to a reserved encoding (VILL=1, VLMUL=4 reserved, SEW > ELEN) via KVM_SET_ONE_REG while keeping vl at a non-zero value via a separate KVM_SET_ONE_REG call. When kvm_riscv_vcpu_guest_vector_restore later restores guest V context, __vstate_csr_restore materializes these inconsistent values onto the guest VCPU hardware. This is the KVM analogue of the signal-restore injection path.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/vector.h:167-194  __vstate_csr_restore
      Issues 'vsetvl x0, %2, %1' with vtype and vl from the in-memory struct, with no software-level double-check that vl has been zeroed for invalid vtype. Relies entirely on hardware vsetvl to sanitize: the ISA spec mandates vsetvl sets vl=0 when vtype is illegal, but there is no software defense-in-depth. Compare with __riscv_v_vstate_discard (line 270) which proactively sets vtype to an illegal encoding (bit 63) to force vl=0 — showing developers were aware of the need for explicit sanitization. This is the single convergence point for ALL restore paths: context switch, signal return, and KVM guest entry.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/ptrace.c:131-215  invalid_ptrace_v_csr
      The ONLY validation function for vtype/vl combinations in the entire kernel. Correctly checks reserved bits (line 149-152), SEW bounds (154-159), VLMUL reserved values (164-170), and VLMAX bounds (172-185). However, it is (a) only used by the ptrace VREGSET setter (riscv_vr_set at line 238), and (b) it rejects invalid combinations rather than sanitizing (doesn't proactively zero vl when vtype is bad). This validation logic should be shared with signal and KVM paths but isn't — this function is evidence of the asymmetry.
